 {"id":"nubian-communities","title":"Nubian communities and languages","period":"Ancient to present","region":"Nile Valley in southern Egypt and northern Sudan","category":"Peoples & cultures","summary":"Nubian is an umbrella term used for communities with deep histories along the Middle Nile. Nubian languages include several varieties, and identities, dialects and cultural practices differ across communities and locations. Modern Nubian life has also been shaped by displacement associated with major dam projects and changing state borders.","status":"Research overview — expand with community voices","source_leads":[{"name":"UNESCO — Nubian monuments and the international campaign","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/activities/172/"},{"name":"Endangered Languages Project","url":"https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/"}],"note":"Do not treat Nubian identity or language as uniform. Add specific community names, self-identifications, oral histories and locally grounded scholarship."},
 {"id":"beja-communities","title":"Beja communities of the Red Sea region","period":"Ancient to present","region":"Eastern Sudan and neighbouring parts of the Red Sea region","category":"Peoples & cultures","summary":"Beja communities have long-standing histories in the Red Sea Hills and adjoining regions. Beja languages belong to the Cushitic branch of the Afroasiatic language family. Pastoralism, trade, mobility and changing political boundaries have all shaped regional histories, with varied experiences among Beja groups.","status":"Research overview — verify subgroup detail","source_leads":[{"name":"Encyclopaedia Britannica — Beja","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Beja-people"},{"name":"Endangered Languages Project","url":"https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/"}],"note":"Avoid presenting all Beja communities as culturally or politically identical; verify subgroup names and preferred self-designations with specialist sources."},
 {"id":"fur-communities","title":"Fur communities and the history of Darfur","period":"Precolonial to present","region":"Darfur, western Sudan","category":"Peoples & cultures","summary":"The Fur are among the communities whose histories are central to Darfur. The historical Sultanate of Darfur was an important regional polity before its incorporation into the expanding Turco-Egyptian state in the nineteenth century. Fur experiences and identities are diverse and have been deeply affected by modern conflict and displacement.","status":"Research overview — source each historical period","source_leads":[{"name":"Encyclopaedia Britannica — Fur","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Fur-people"},{"name":"UNESCO — Darfur and cultural heritage resources","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en"}],"note":"Darfur is home to many communities, not only the Fur. Do not conflate ethnic identity, political allegiance or the experiences of all residents."},
 {"id":"nuba-communities","title":"Nuba peoples of the Nuba Mountains","period":"Ancient to present","region":"Nuba Mountains, South Kordofan, Sudan","category":"Peoples & cultures","summary":"Nuba is a broad label for numerous communities in the Nuba Mountains, with substantial linguistic and cultural diversity. Communities have distinct histories, local identities and social traditions. The region's modern history includes conflict, displacement and political marginalisation.","status":"Research overview — diversity must remain visible","source_leads":[{"name":"Minority Rights Group — Sudan","url":"https://minorityrights.org/country/sudan/"},{"name":"Endangered Languages Project","url":"https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/"}],"note":"Never describe the Nuba as one single ethnic group or language. Build separate entries only where reliable, community-specific evidence is available."},
 {"id":"dinka-communities","title":"Dinka communities and histories","period":"Historical records to present","region":"Multiple regions of South Sudan, especially the Bahr el Ghazal and Upper Nile areas","category":"Peoples & cultures","summary":"Dinka (often self-referred to by the name Jieng in some contexts) encompasses multiple communities with distinct local histories and identities. Dinka languages belong to the Nilotic branch of the Eastern Sudanic language family. Cattle have important social, economic and cultural roles in many Dinka communities, though practices vary.","status":"Research overview — avoid generalisation","source_leads":[{"name":"Encyclopaedia Britannica — Dinka","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Dinka"},{"name":"Minority Rights Group — South Sudan","url":"https://minorityrights.org/country/south-sudan/"}],"note":"Do not reduce Dinka society to cattle culture or imply all Dinka communities share one political position, dialect or custom."},
 {"id":"nuer-communities","title":"Nuer communities and histories","period":"Historical records to present","region":"South Sudan and adjacent areas of western Ethiopia","category":"Peoples & cultures","summary":"Nuer communities have histories across parts of what is now South Sudan and western Ethiopia. Nuer languages are Nilotic. Livelihoods have included pastoralism, cultivation, fishing and trade, shaped by seasonal environments and local histories. Political and social organisation varies across places and periods.","status":"Research overview — avoid generalisation","source_leads":[{"name":"Encyclopaedia Britannica — Nuer","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Nuer"},{"name":"Minority Rights Group — South Sudan","url":"https://minorityrights.org/country/south-sudan/"}],"note":"Use ethnographic sources critically and historically; do not treat older colonial-era descriptions as timeless or definitive."},
 {"id":"shilluk-communities","title":"Shilluk (Chollo) communities and the kingdom of the Shilluk","period":"At least several centuries to present","region":"Western bank of the White Nile, especially around the Upper Nile region of South Sudan","category":"Peoples & cultures","summary":"The Shilluk, also known as Chollo, have a distinctive history associated with a kingdom along the White Nile. Their political institutions, oral traditions and relations with neighbouring communities have changed over time. Shilluk language belongs to the Nilotic language family.","status":"Research overview — oral and written sources needed","source_leads":[{"name":"Encyclopaedia Britannica — Shilluk","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Shilluk"},{"name":"Minority Rights Group — South Sudan","url":"https://minorityrights.org/country/south-sudan/"}],"note":"Record Chollo/Shilluk naming carefully and include oral histories alongside documentary and academic sources, identifying the nature and date of each source."}

