 {"id":"south-sudan-precolonial-longue-duree","title":"South Sudan before the nineteenth century: landscapes, archaeology and regional histories","period":"c. 3000 BCE–c. 1800 CE; chronology varies by region","region":"The territory of present-day South Sudan and connected Upper Nile borderlands","category":"Full historical chapters","summary":"A detailed, evidence-led chapter on the deep history of the Upper Nile: archaeology, environment, food production, ironworking, mobility, oral traditions, political institutions and regional diversity before nineteenth-century conquest and colonial rule. It distinguishes what archaeological evidence demonstrates from what oral histories and later written sources suggest.","status":"Research chapter — evidence is uneven and dates are provisional","article_sections":[
 ["1. A historical region before it was a country","The modern Republic of South Sudan became independent in 2011. Its present borders are therefore a recent political framework, not the boundaries of an ancient kingdom or a single precolonial society. Before the nineteenth century, the lands now inside those borders formed part of a much wider Upper Nile world. Rivers, seasonal wetlands, savannas, forests, cattle routes, fishing grounds and trade paths connected communities across areas that are now divided between South Sudan, Sudan, Ethiopia, Uganda, Kenya, the Central African Republic and the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Political and cultural relationships did not stop at the lines later drawn by colonial administrations. A history of this region must therefore examine both local developments and the networks that linked them to neighbouring areas. It must also resist treating today's national population as if it had always shared one political identity, one language or one origin story. The historical record reveals many communities, changing alliances and distinct local trajectories."],
 ["2. The land, rivers and seasonal environment","The White Nile and its tributaries are central to the region's geography, but the landscape is not simply a river valley. The Sudd, one of the world's largest tropical wetlands, expands and contracts with seasonal water flows. The Bahr el Jebel, Bahr el Ghazal, Sobat and other river systems connect wetlands to drier grasslands and upland areas. Rainfall is strongly seasonal, and the availability of pasture, fish, cultivable land and safe routes changes during the year. These conditions influenced settlement, food production, transport, exchange and conflict, but they did not mechanically determine social organisation. Communities developed varied ways to manage environmental uncertainty: some combined cultivation and livestock keeping; others relied on fishing, hunting, gathering, trade or different combinations of these activities. Floods and droughts could create hardship, but wetlands also supported rich fisheries, grazing and transport. Historical explanations should describe the decisions people made within these environments rather than portray the landscape as an obstacle that prevented history."],
 ["3. What archaeology can—and cannot—tell us","Archaeology provides the most important independent evidence for the deep past of the territory that is now South Sudan. A peer-reviewed review by David Kay, Samuel Lunn-Rockliffe and Matthew Davies surveys archaeological research from about 3000 BCE to 1500 CE and emphasises both the diversity of the material record and the severe limits created by uneven survey and excavation. Archaeological remains include settlement traces, ceramics, stone tools, metalworking evidence, burial places and material associated with changing subsistence practices. These finds can establish that people lived in particular places, used particular technologies and participated in wider patterns of exchange. They do not automatically reveal the names those people used for themselves, the languages they spoke or whether they belonged to a present-day ethnic community. It is especially important not to assign an archaeological culture directly to a modern people without a clear chain of evidence. The record is geographically uneven: a lack of excavated sites in one area is not proof that the area was empty."],
 ["4. Communities and lifeways in the third and second millennia BCE","The archaeological review identifies the third millennium BCE as an important period for investigating the region's early communities and their relationships with broader northeast African developments. The evidence is not a complete narrative of successive peoples. Instead, it points to changing and sometimes coexisting lifeways, including hunting and gathering, cultivation, animal keeping and the movement of people and materials. The spread of pastoralism across eastern and northeastern Africa was a long process rather than a single migration event. Communities could adopt livestock keeping while retaining other food strategies, and different groups could use the same landscape in complementary or competing ways. Northern Nubian kingdoms such as Kerma were developing farther up the Nile during part of this broad period, but the degree and nature of their contact with communities in the territory of modern South Sudan remain questions for research. It would be misleading to assume that the political boundaries or cultural influence of Kerma extended uniformly far to the south."],
 ["5. Ironworking, farming and changing technologies","The period from the later first millennium BCE into the first millennium CE is important for studying the development and spread of ironworking and changing patterns of agriculture and settlement. The archaeological record across the region is still limited, but excavated and surveyed evidence suggests diverse local histories rather than one uniform technological transition. Iron tools could support farming, woodworking and other activities, while iron production itself required knowledge, fuel, labour and access to suitable ores. The appearance of iron objects at a site does not by itself prove that iron was smelted there; objects may have arrived through exchange. Likewise, pottery styles and settlement forms should be interpreted in their archaeological context. The available evidence raises questions about how technical knowledge travelled, how communities adapted it and how local production related to regional exchange. More survey, excavation, scientific dating and publication by researchers working in South Sudan are needed before detailed regional chronologies can be treated as settled."],
 ["6. The Nile corridor and the limits of ancient written sources","Written references to areas south of Meroë are sparse and often come from outsiders. Greek and Roman authors recorded accounts of expeditions and reports about the Nile and regions farther south, but such descriptions were shaped by the interests and geographical knowledge of their writers. The archaeological review by Kay and colleagues notes that the documentary evidence for the region is extremely limited for long periods and that even reported expeditions require critical evaluation. A text that names a river, ruler or distant people does not necessarily provide reliable information about everyday life across the Upper Nile. Nor should a classical geographical label be mapped automatically onto a modern ethnic or national identity. The Meroitic kingdom and later Nubian states formed part of the wider northern context, but direct political control over all lands now within South Sudan cannot be assumed. Where the evidence does not establish a connection, the responsible account says so."],
 ["7. Oral histories as historical evidence","Oral traditions are essential to the history of South Sudan, especially where written records are scarce. They may preserve accounts of migration, settlement, relations between communities, leadership, spiritual authority, conflict, alliance and changes in the environment. They are not simply stories to be placed below written documents in a hierarchy of evidence. Like written records, oral accounts have authors, audiences, purposes and histories of transmission. Different narrators may remember the same event differently; a story can change as communities respond to new political circumstances. The historian must record who told an account, where and when it was recorded, in which language, for what purpose and under what conditions. Douglas H. Johnson has argued that colonial-era collections often contain officials' summaries rather than carefully attributed testimony, making it difficult to separate an informant's account from the collector's assumptions. Comparing separately recorded versions can reveal both historical memories and the contexts in which those memories were shaped."],
 ["8. Migration, settlement and the problem of fixed origins","Many communities in the Upper Nile preserve traditions about movement from earlier homelands, encounters with other groups and the founding of settlements. Linguistic comparison, oral traditions, archaeological evidence and environmental history can all contribute to the study of these movements. However, proposed dates and routes are often disputed. Migrations usually unfolded over generations and could involve small groups, intermarriage, adoption of language, political incorporation and repeated movement—not necessarily a single mass arrival. The boundaries of communities could also change as people joined new social and political networks. A shared language does not prove that every speaker descended from one recent migrating population; similarly, a community's present name may not have been used in the same way centuries ago. NILE HISTORY will therefore present specific migration traditions as attributed accounts and scholarly hypotheses, not as unquestionable facts about the biological or cultural origins of entire peoples."],
 ["9. Political authority: kingdoms, chiefdoms and other institutions","Precolonial South Sudan did not have one political system. The historical landscape included centralised kingship, local leadership, councils, lineage-based authority, spiritual offices and other institutions whose powers varied across communities and periods. The Shilluk kingdom on the western bank of the White Nile is a prominent example of a centralised royal institution. Its history should be studied through Shilluk/Chollo traditions, historical scholarship and the records of neighbouring states, rather than reduced to a list of kings. Other communities developed political arrangements that did not resemble a territorial monarchy. Older colonial writers sometimes described such societies as 'stateless' or lacking government. Those labels can obscure the ways authority was exercised through age sets, local leaders, assemblies, kinship, ritual specialists, negotiation and customary law. The absence of a palace or central bureaucracy is not the absence of politics, law or historical change."],
 ["10. The Shilluk/Chollo kingdom and the western Nile","The Shilluk, who also use the name Chollo, developed a distinctive kingdom along the western bank of the White Nile. Its royal institution became an important part of the political history of the Upper Nile and its relations with neighbouring communities. The kingdom's formation and chronology are reconstructed from oral traditions and later documentary sources, and individual details require careful source criticism. The history should not be treated as a timeless system: royal authority, succession, relations with local communities and external political pressures changed over time. The Shilluk case also demonstrates why oral histories must be taken seriously. They preserve political memory and accounts of authority, but different versions may emphasise different ancestors, events or legitimising claims. A fuller chapter should include Chollo-language terminology, locally authorised oral testimony and scholarship by historians who have worked directly with Shilluk sources."],
 ["11. Dinka, Nuer, Anyuak, Bari and other regional histories","The territory of modern South Sudan is home to many communities, including Dinka, Nuer, Anyuak, Bari, Azande, Murle, Lotuko, Toposa, Jie, Kuku, Kakwa, Madi, Mundari, Bongo, Shilluk/Chollo and many others. This list is illustrative, not exhaustive, and the names used for communities may vary by language, locality and historical source. Dinka and Nuer histories include patterns of movement, intermarriage, alliance, cattle exchange, competition over resources and conflict. Research by Douglas H. Johnson has challenged older descriptions that present Dinka and Nuer relations as nothing but permanent hostility; local relationships could be cooperative and interconnected, and political identities could cross presumed ethnic boundaries. The Anyuak have histories across the Sobat region and the Ethiopia–South Sudan borderlands. Bari communities have histories along the White Nile and surrounding areas. Azande political history connects southwestern South Sudan with neighbouring parts of Central Africa. Each community requires its own chronology and sources. No single ethnographic description can stand for all communities or all periods."],
 ["12. Livelihoods, cattle, cultivation and exchange","Cattle have been important in many Upper Nile societies, but their significance cannot be reduced to food or wealth alone. In particular contexts cattle have been connected to marriage arrangements, social relationships, ritual, prestige, compensation and obligations between families. The exact practices vary and have changed over time. Cattle keeping has often existed alongside cultivation, fishing, hunting, gathering and trade. Seasonal movement can be a deliberate way to use pasture and water rather than evidence of aimless wandering. Markets and exchange connected communities that colonial administrators sometimes portrayed as isolated. The history of the region must also consider women's work, food processing, cultivation, household production, trade and knowledge transmission, which are often underrepresented in older male-authored accounts. Economic life was diverse and historically changing; it should not be described through one stereotyped image of a cattle society."],
 ["13. Neighbouring states, trade and external pressure","Communities in the Upper Nile interacted with states and trading networks to the north, east, west and south. The nature of these connections changed over time and was not always direct political rule. Goods, religious ideas, technologies, people and information could move through multiple intermediaries. The Meroitic state, Christian Nubian kingdoms, the Funj Sultanate, Ethiopian polities and Central African states belong to the wider regional history, but the existence of these neighbours does not prove that they governed every community in the modern territory of South Sudan. The growth of long-distance trade in the nineteenth century, including the expansion of slave raiding and ivory commerce, brought profound changes and violence to many areas. That later history must be studied in its own detail and with careful attention to the different actors involved; it should not be projected backwards as the defining feature of all earlier centuries."],
 ["14. How historians should read colonial-era ethnography","Many written descriptions of southern Sudanese societies were produced by nineteenth- and twentieth-century travellers, missionaries, soldiers and colonial administrators. Such records can preserve valuable observations, names, vocabulary and dates, but they were created within unequal political relationships. Officials often organised communities into fixed 'tribes', treated flexible identities as permanent, or described political institutions using European assumptions about statehood. Some accounts were based on interpreters or informants whose names and circumstances were not recorded. A careful historian compares these documents with oral testimony, local-language sources, archaeology, linguistics and later scholarship. Colonial records should be cited as records of what an author or administration claimed, not automatically as objective descriptions of an entire people. Where an old source uses outdated or offensive terminology, NILE HISTORY should explain the historical context and use current community-preferred names in its own narration."],
 ["15. The nineteenth-century transition and the next chapter","By the early nineteenth century, the region's communities already had long and varied histories. The expansion of Turco-Egyptian power from the north, commercial networks, ivory and slave trading, armed expeditions and later European colonial rule would transform political and economic life. These processes did not affect every locality at the same moment or in the same way. Local leaders and communities responded through negotiation, resistance, alliance, adaptation and movement. The next chapter in NILE HISTORY should examine these transformations region by region, distinguishing the Turco-Egyptian conquest of northern Sudan from the later and uneven extension of external power into southern territories. It should also document the experiences of people who were enslaved, displaced or forced into new economic systems, while avoiding the claim that the nineteenth century created the region's history. It changed societies that already had deep pasts."],
 ["16. Evidence gaps and priorities for future research","The archaeological record remains uneven, and political instability has interrupted research, conservation and access to collections. Oral histories have not been collected systematically from every community, and many existing records lack clear information about narrators, dates, language and consent. Some colonial archives are extensive but reflect the priorities of the administrations that created them. Research priorities include community-led oral history, local-language publication, archaeological survey and scientific dating, preservation of manuscripts and recordings, women's histories, histories of trade and technology, and documentation of smaller or underrepresented communities. Sudanese and South Sudanese scholars, heritage workers, elders, language specialists and community organisations must be recognised as knowledge producers, not merely as sources for outside researchers. New evidence may revise this chapter; that is a strength of historical research, not a failure."],
 ["17. Conclusion: a region of connected histories","The deep history of South Sudan is not an empty prelude to colonialism, civil war or independence. Archaeology, oral tradition, linguistics and regional history show a landscape inhabited and shaped by communities over many centuries. People developed varied economies, technologies and political institutions, moved and formed new relationships, and participated in networks that crossed the boundaries of the modern state. Some details remain uncertain because evidence is scarce or uneven. The answer is not to fill those gaps with invented certainty, but to explain what is known, identify competing interpretations and keep researching. A comprehensive national history must make room for the diversity of local histories while showing how those histories became connected in the modern era."]
 ],"article_text":["The history of the lands that became South Sudan begins long before the creation of the modern state in 2011. Before the nineteenth century, communities across the Upper Nile lived in a connected but highly diverse region shaped by rivers, wetlands, savannas, forests, trade routes and seasonal movement. No single kingdom or ethnic identity covered the whole territory. Archaeology, oral traditions, linguistics and neighbouring written sources each illuminate parts of this past, but none provides a complete record on its own.","A peer-reviewed archaeological review by David Kay, Samuel Lunn-Rockliffe and Matthew Davies surveys evidence from about 3000 BCE to 1500 CE. It highlights changing lifeways, including pastoralism, cultivation and ironworking, while stressing how limited archaeological coverage remains. Finds can document settlement and technology, but cannot automatically be assigned to a modern ethnic group. The modern borders of South Sudan must not be projected backwards as ancient political boundaries.","The Shilluk/Chollo kingdom is an important example of centralised kingship in the Upper Nile. Other communities developed different forms of authority, including local leadership, councils, age-based institutions, lineage networks and spiritual offices. Older colonial labels such as 'stateless' often obscure these institutions. Dinka, Nuer, Anyuak, Bari, Azande, Murle, Lotuko, Toposa and many other communities have distinct histories that require separate, locally grounded treatment.","Oral histories are indispensable, but must be recorded with the narrator, language, locality, date and circumstances. Douglas H. Johnson has shown why colonial-era summaries of oral traditions need critical assessment: they may combine an informant's account with the assumptions of the official who recorded it. Oral accounts should be compared with one another and with archaeology, linguistics and written sources, not dismissed or treated as timeless statements.","The nineteenth century brought major transformations through Turco-Egyptian expansion, commercial networks, ivory and slave trading, armed expeditions and later colonial rule. These processes changed societies that already had deep histories. A full account must examine regional differences, local agency, resistance, adaptation and the experiences of people affected by violence and displacement."],"source_leads":[{"name":"Kay, Lunn-Rockliffe & Davies (2019), The archaeology of South Sudan from c. 3000 BC to AD 1500 — Cambridge Repository, accepted peer-reviewed version","url":"https://www.repository.cam.ac.uk/items/1767f474-8215-46af-963e-c40a12b7ff0b"},{"name":"Johnson, Douglas H. (2016), South Sudan: A New History for a New Nation — Ohio University Press Open Access","url":"https://ohioopen.library.ohio.edu/oupress/76/"},{"name":"Johnson, Douglas H. (1982), Ngundeng and the Turuk: Two Narratives Compared — History in Africa","url":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/history-in-africa/article/abs/ngundeng-and-the-turuk-two-narratives-compared/38CAF1CF6CDE1B92966800C8F55B7D97"},{"name":"Rolandsen & Daly, A History of South Sudan — Cambridge University Press excerpt on Upper Nile societies and historical sources","url":"https://assets.cambridge.org/97805211/16312/excerpt/9780521116312_excerpt.pdf"},{"name":"Kay, Lunn-Rockliffe & Davies (2019), open accepted manuscript — UCL Discovery","url":"https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10090610/"}],"note":"Original NILE HISTORY chapter synthesised from the cited archaeological review and historical scholarship. This is not a claim that every community's history is fully covered. Dates and migration interpretations remain provisional where evidence is limited. The Cambridge archaeology manuscript is described as CC BY-NC 4.0 in the repository; this chapter is an independent synthesis, not a reproduction of that paper. The Ohio University Press book is CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 and is linked rather than copied or adapted."}
